{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1779398163,"nonce":"KA4jFpQJnD1SaBa5ki7oOJ7GzGc=","metadata":{"github-id":"UCE_lAHOK4fCvM8AAAABDBjzH869MoYF"},"target":"001461d084e6d4d14e94ddc72dc66a644befe23e841ccb89d3b68f2e9d0c887b","message":"## Problem\n\nSurfaced from 2026-05-21 deployment debrief: video quality on lossy paths (OTH, Starlink-only, fringe-range WiFi) takes a long time to recover after packet loss because the encoder settings are configured for bandwidth efficiency rather than loss resilience. A short loss event garbles many subsequent inter-frames before the next keyframe lets the decoder resync.\n\nTwo encoder-side levers interact and are best tuned together:\n\n1. **Keyframe interval.** Shorter interval → faster post-loss recovery → higher bandwidth (keyframes are typically several times larger than inter-frames).\n2. **Resolution.** Lower resolution → less bandwidth across the board → fewer dropped frames + faster recovery → but less detail for operator situational awareness (the perceptual / UX trade-off).\n\nThese levers compose. Reducing resolution may dominate the bandwidth effect and make the keyframe-interval choice less stressful; or shrinking keyframes at lower resolution may give a different sweet spot than at full resolution. A single measurement campaign that varies both is much cheaper than two campaigns that vary one at a time.\n\n## Task\n\nMeasurement-driven joint tuning of keyframe interval × resolution, in this order:\n\n1. Identify the current keyframe-interval and resolution settings in the bizzy OAK launch / encoder config (likely in `bizzyboat_project11/launch/` or wherever the OAK pipeline is configured — needs source-code verification before claiming the location).\n2. Pick a small Pareto-style grid of settings:\n   - 2–3 keyframe intervals (e.g. 30, 15, 8 frames at the current frame rate)\n   - 2–3 resolutions spanning a useful range (e.g. current, 1/2 area, 1/4 area)\n   - That's 4–9 cells; pick a useful subset rather than the full grid.\n3. **Bag-side preview first** (saves field time): use existing 2026-05-21 bag imagery to downsample to the candidate resolutions and estimate bandwidth at each setting offline (see [`#150 topic 8`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/150) which sets this up). Eliminates obviously-bad settings before any field test.\n4. Capture short OTH or Starlink-only segments at the surviving candidate settings (could fold into the next deployment or use a bench-side lossy-link emulator if available).\n5. Compare across:\n   - Visual recovery time after a representative packet loss\n   - Bandwidth used per camera per second\n   - **Operator-perceived quality** at the lower resolutions — qualitative criterion that needs operator-in-the-loop evaluation. At what resolution does the operator still feel they can see far enough ahead, spot floating obstacles, read the dock approach? Resolution affects this in a way that keyframe interval doesn't.\n6. Pick the (keyframe, resolution) point that balances those for class-day OTH use; document in the camera config with a comment explaining the choice.\n\n## Why now\n\nLake Massabesic surveys go OTH routinely. Operator situational awareness from cameras is one of the key safety nets when the planner doesn't have obstacle avoidance (per the 2026-05-21 collision debrief). A long video-recovery latency turns a 200 ms packet loss into 5+ s of blind operation, and a too-fat per-camera bitrate squeezes other essential topics off the link.\n\n## References\n\n- Source debrief: see [`unh_echoboats_project11#149`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/149) and the 2026-05-21 dev log entries around 17:05 + 17:12 EDT.\n- Bag-side prep work: [`#150 topic 8`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/150) — preview the resolution effect on existing bag imagery before any field measurement.\n- Coordinate with: [`#152`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/152) (rear camera over VPN) — same encoder + bridge surface; doing them together avoids round-tripping.\n- Roadmap: \"Over-horizon operations capability\" — topic-budget cull intersects this; faster keyframes / lower resolution change which topics dominate the link.\n\n## Anti-scope\n\nThis issue is about the **encoder-side keyframe interval and resolution**. Not about the bridge/QoS layer (that's a separate concern). Not about a hardware codec change. Not about adding new cameras (rear camera enable is [`#152`](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/152)).\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null},{"type":2,"timestamp":1779398163,"nonce":"5h8taB9/Wxj6uj/VjAoeVfDNpr0=","metadata":{"github-id":"RTE_lADOK4fCvM8AAAABDBjzH88AAAAGA0m0bA"},"title":"OAK camera encoder tuning — joint keyframe + resolution measurement campaign for OTH","was":"OAK camera encoder tuning — joint keyframe + resolution measurement campaign for OTH"}]}